This incorporates the probability of grain growth direction based on probability and thermal gradient. Thus, growth is no longer guaranteed purely on the basis of thermal gradient.
The current rule stated that if the temperature is less than the melting temperature and a solid neighbour exists, then the growth starts. This means that the boundary velocity is infinite and equal for all cells. However, we need to consider the growth speed based on thermal differential as well. Thus, probability of growth depends on undercooling
A major thing to note here is the use of np.random.random()<P_growth in the last condition. Here, it may happen that grains with high probability (~0.9 ,0.95) may also not grow merely because of random number generation where the random number exceeds them. This may inhibit the growth of cells that should have grown ideally having very high probability of growth based on temperature differential. This randomness is something that is still averaged out as a cumulative picture but still affects individual cell. Reason to incorporate this was stated as local kinetics are noisy and interface attachment is statistical, not deterministic. So it does indirectly includes the thermal motion affecting grain growth.
